---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { armImages, siteData } from '../lib/site';

const site = await siteData();
const { devices } = await armImages();
const desktop = devices.filter((d) => !d.server);
const servers = devices.filter((d) => d.server);
---
<Base
  title={`EndeavourOS ARM — ${site.name}`}
  description="EndeavourOS images for Raspberry Pi, Odroid and PineBook Pro, and how to install them."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container stack">
      <h1>EndeavourOS ARM</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        EndeavourOS runs on ARM single-board computers and laptops as a community-driven
        release. Pick an image for your device, verify it, and write it to a storage device.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--sunken">
    <div class="container stack">
      <h2 id="images">Download ARM images</h2>
      <table class="mirrors mirrors--arm">
        <caption class="sr-only">
          EndeavourOS ARM images by device. Each row links to the image and to its sha512
          checksum.
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Device</th>
            <th scope="col">Image</th>
            <th scope="col">Verify</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {desktop.map((d) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="c-loc">{d.name}</th>
              <td class="c-name">
                <a href={d.img} rel="noopener noreferrer">
                  {d.image}<span class="sr-only"> — download the {d.name} image</span>
                </a>
              </td>
              <td class="c-verify">
                <a href={d.sha512} rel="noopener noreferrer">
                  sha512<span class="sr-only"> checksum for {d.name}</span>
                </a>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 id="server-images">Headless server images</h3>
      <p>
        The server images install exactly as the desktop ones do, with one difference noted
        below: write them to a micro SD card only.
      </p>
      <table class="mirrors mirrors--arm">
        <caption class="sr-only">Headless server images by device.</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Device</th>
            <th scope="col">Image</th>
            <th scope="col">Verify</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {servers.map((d) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="c-loc">{d.name}</th>
              <td class="c-name">
                <a href={d.img} rel="noopener noreferrer">
                  {d.image}<span class="sr-only"> — download the {d.name} image</span>
                </a>
              </td>
              <td class="c-verify">
                <a href={d.sha512} rel="noopener noreferrer">
                  sha512<span class="sr-only"> checksum for {d.name}</span>
                </a>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container stack">
      <h2 id="install">Installation</h2>
      <p>
        Download both files for your device — the image and its <code>.sha512sum</code>. In a
        terminal, change to the directory they were downloaded to and verify the image:
      </p>
      <pre><code>sha512sum -c enosLinuxARM-rpi5-latest.img.xz.sha512sum</code></pre>
      <p>
        That must come back <code>OK</code>. If it does not, the download is damaged or
        tampered with — fetch it again rather than writing it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Write the verified image to a storage device — a micro SD card, eMMC module or USB SSD
        — with whichever flashing tool you prefer. Connect it to your ARM device and boot.
      </p>
      <p>
        The device logs in automatically and runs a setup script that asks for your details.
        Answer its questions; when it finishes, the device reboots into a working desktop or
        window manager.
      </p>

      <h3 id="server-install">Headless server setup</h3>
      <p>
        Follow the steps above, but write the server image to a micro SD card only. Connect
        the card to a Raspberry Pi 4b or 5b that has a monitor, keyboard and mouse attached.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you want the script to partition and format a data drive, connect a USB 3 SSD to a
        USB 3 port before booting. It will create the mount points and add them
        to <code>/etc/fstab</code> so the drive mounts on every boot.
      </p>
      <p>
        Boot the Pi and answer the configuration prompts. From the second boot onward you can
        remove the monitor, keyboard and mouse and run the server headless.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          The <a href="https://discovery.endeavouros.com/category/arm/" rel="noopener noreferrer">ARM
          articles in Discovery</a> carry the rest: Homeserver 1, 2, 3 and 7 walk through
          setting up a Linux LAN file server, and there are further how-tos for SAMBA and
          miniDLNA.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
